-- OWNERSHIP fixture for rule-stdlib-reimpl (#2248 Miss 1, F1-ownership-basename-fix).
-- This file's BASENAME is `list` -- the same module id the real stdlib uses for
-- `list.reverse` -- but the file is NOT under `stdlib/`. It therefore owns
-- nothing, and the rule MUST fire.
--
-- That is the whole point of the fixture. The ownership exemption originally
-- shipped keyed on `modIdOf path` ALONE, with no `isStdlibPath` conjunct, which
-- re-created #2248 Miss 1 one level down: any file anywhere named `list.mdk`
-- became blanket-exempt from every `list` export. This fixture was then written
-- with an EMPTY golden, enshrining that bug as the expected answer. Ownership
-- needs both conjuncts -- under `stdlib/` AND the matching module id -- so the
-- expectation here is a finding, not silence.
--
-- NOTE: the positive half (the true owner, `stdlib/list.mdk`, staying silent on
-- its own `reverse`) is NOT expressible in this corpus: `diff_compiler_lint.sh`
-- globs `test/lint_fixtures/*.mdk` flat, and no path under that directory has a
-- `stdlib` path segment. It is covered by the contract's E1 check instead.
export
reverse : List a -> List a
reverse [] = []
reverse (x::xs) = reverse xs ++ [x]
